  "question": {
    "text": "Let $S(K)=1+3+5+\\ldots+(2 K-1)=3+K^2$. Then which of the following is true?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "5ZsIo",
      "text": "\n$S(1)$ is correct\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "FtUTD",
      "text": "\nPrinciple of mathematical induction can be used to prove the formula\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Kz4Ov",
      "text": "\n$S(K) \\neq S(K+1)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ufbFo",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{S}(\\mathrm{K}) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{S}(\\mathrm{K}+1)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& S(k)=1+3+5+\\ldots \\ldots . .+(2 k-1)=3+k^2 \\\\\n& S(k+1)=1+3+5+\\ldots \\ldots \\ldots+(2 k-1)+(2 k+1) \\\\\n& =\\left(3+k^2\\right)+2 k+1=k^2+2 k+4\\left[\\text { from } S(k)=3+k^2\\right] \\\\\n& =3+\\left(k^2+2 k+1\\right)=3+(k+1)^2=S(k+1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nAlthough $S(k)$ in itself is not true but it considered true will always imply towards $S(k+1)$.",
    "image": null
  },
